  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 308 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTuesday and Wednesday will remain very warm, with highs well into\nthe mid 90s across most of west central Texas. Meanwhile, an \nupper level low pressure is forecast to deepen over the southern \nRockies on Tuesday and Wednesday. In response, the upper level \nridge over Texas begins to flatten out and shift to the east. \nRain chances enter the forecast on Wednesday as southwest flow \naloft becomes established over west Texas. Models have come into a\nlittle better agreement with the progression of the upper trough \nfor the middle of the week, ejecting it out across the Central \nPlains and upper Mississippi Valley on Thursday and Friday \nalthough the ECMWF remains a bit slower compared to the GFS. \nRegardless, both models bring a cold front southward into the area\nThursday night into Friday morning. Rain chances go up on \nThursday and Thursday night as the cold front approaches and large\nscale forcing for ascent increases. Given the very moist airmass \nin place (PW values approaching 2 inches) locally heavy rainfall \nwill be possible on Thursday night. Rain chances taper off by \nFriday afternoon as much drier air filters into the region behind \nthe front. Highs next weekend will potentially only top out in the\n60s, with lows dipping down into the 40s on Saturday.",
